Leigh Porter (leigh@wisper.net) wrote:
: I am thinking about buying a Microway 566Mhz Screamer box but wondered a
: few things...
I was worried about this too so I bought an inexpensive ($2100) 533 MHz SX
box from dcg (www.dcginc.com, but Microway sells a similar box). I was
able to easily integrate into our environment (mostly DEC alpha boxes
running DUX, but also linux/x86 and Sun Ultra). So far (only 2 days) it has
been quite stable. I have had no trouble compiling and running my own
programs on it.
: Are they fast (Faster than a Pentium 2 300?)
Judging from the SPECfp ratings they should be at least 2x as fast as a single
Pentium 2 300 on FP work. Comparing the above system to a PPro 200, I see
a difference of about 50% using gcc and more than a factor of 2 using the
DEC C compilers (I don't know how well the Microway compilers would work).
The 566 MHz screamer should be even faster since it has the fast on chip L2
96 MB cache that the 21164PC based systems don't have and since it is 5%
faster clock.
: Is Linux stable on them,?
It has been stable so far.
: Does X work good with the Matrox Millenium 2 card?
From what I have read it does, but I have a S3 based card in mine.
: Do most things compile out of the box on Alpha (Are there any
: endian-nessy things or 32/64bit problems)?
There should be no endian problems as it is the same as Intel. The
problems may be due to pointer sizes ( alpha uses 64 bit pointers)
If you write portable code (don't assume pointers can be cast as integers,
that size of pointers are 4 bytes, etc) then in my experience there are
few problems.

: Would you buy one!? (This kinda has to be the ultimate question!?).
I did. But I was hedging my bets by buying a cheap one now to evaluate
and if it works we may make a larger investment in the future. So far things
look good.

