On Tue, 16 Dec 1997 12:31:02 -0500, Charles M Richmond <cmr@iisc.com> wrote:
>
>> >Multias are old and very slow, but I think the most important reason
>> >why everybody think they are DOG SLOW is that most of them only have
>> >24 MB of ram (and 256 kB of cache). Let's face it people, 64 MB is the
>> >absolute minimum for Linux/Alpha, at least if you're using X and other
>> >memory-hog prorams. 96 MB would be better and 128 MB quite ok. So
>> >at least give those cute Multia/UDB/Noname babies a chance and
>> >do a RAM upgrade before dumping them. :-)
>> >
>
>The 233 has a much improved cache and that when combined with the CPU
>speed muchly improves performance. I do agree that the memory needs
>to be added to. My system was a full system which means that it came
>with the peculiar 12M SIMMS. I added 2 16M SIMMS to bring the total 
>to 56M. Performance is fine and 'free' returns:
>
>             total       used       free     shared    buffers    
>cached
>Mem:         53632      52120       1512      65512       4432     
>19336
>-/+ buffers:            28352      25280
>Swap:       110584          0     110584
>
>Charlie

Yep, I get similar stats running RH 4.2 on 64 megs RAM.  I've got a 9
gig external hard drive attached (and boy does it make a lot of
noise), and it runs slower than a P5/100, yet much faster than a
486DX/100.  That is with all sorts of strange unknown daemons loaded
and stuff (one of the tradeoffs of using a distribution instead of
installing from scratch...).

Here's a benchmark ... it takes about 30 minutes to compile vmlinux.
For programming I'd recommend a faster computer; compilation is slow
(compared to a nice fast pentium or a better alpha), tho for general
or experimental use, the Multia really is very nice.

-Albert



