Since kernel 2.0.30 (or maybe earlier) SYS-V IPC is broken for Alpha in the
vanilla kernels found on sunsite or elsewhere. For 2.0.30 exists a diff
(called axp-diffs-2.0.30) which fixes this and maybe some other things. The
same fault remained in 2.0.31 and no appropriate diff occurred. It also
remained in 2.0.32 and now in 2.0.33 too!
Why is this so?
The bug seems to be known and seems fixable but why does noone care about
it? I don't like the idea to have my Alpha running an experimental kernel
with known bugs and to also know that noone fixes them!
The 2.0.* kernels should be stable production kernels. For Alpha I cannot
say so! The IPC bug prevents the X11 shared memory from working! (Ever seen
the errormessage "Access to private resource denied"? That's exactly it!).

So if anyone of the Alpha core developers should eventually read this please
persuade the kernel source keeper, whoever it might be now, to include those
patches in the vanilla kernels! PLEASE!
From release to release the old 2.0.30 diff gets more and more complicated
to apply...

CU
  nils


