> For programming I'd recommend a faster computer; compilation is slow
> (compared to a nice fast pentium or a better alpha), tho for general
> or experimental use, the Multia really is very nice.
> 
> -Albert
Well, you cannot really benchmark a multia with the compilation
time of the compiler which has an entirely different optmizing
scheme than a that of the P5 compiler.
Two year's back I did some benchmarking with Neural networks
between a 166Mhz multia and a 120Mhz P5 from HP, both running 
Linux.
The multia executed the networks ~1.5 times faster than the HP.
I have to admit that all the code was pure double precision
matrix math, compiled with GNU 2.7.2.
I then tried a GCC compilation under OSF/1 on the multia, which
made the network execute twice as fast. This result made me
drop linux, since I was only interested in the speed.
I really liked linux a lot, but as long as the GNU compiler is
doing such a bad job in floating point optimizing on Alpha,
there's no room for it on my harddisk.

cheers
	Anders Blicher Petersen



