On 12 Dec 1997 11:06:27 GMT, JaNuS <januzz@noord.bart.nl> spake unto us:
>Hello, I bought recently a ALPHA XL-266 at a show
[should he install 4.2 or 5.0?]

I'd strongly recommend installing 5.0.  The libc is much less buggy, the
sh-utils actually log to utmp correctly, XEmacs might even link correctly,
new versions of the compiler (like egcs) will install without the user
going through contortions, and in general it's a better system.

Also consider that RedHat will never upgrade 4.2 above its current status.
Problems will be fixed in 5.0, but who knows if they'll be fixed in 4.2.

Oh, and new kernel versions are going to want to use the new libc as well.

Finally, note that if you find a problem with 4.2, the likely response is
going to be "upgrade to 5.0 to fix the problem".

Most of the problems people have been reporting with 5.0 have been
compatibility issues with 4.2.

-andy
