adisaacs@trurl.resnet.mtu.edu (Andy Isaacson) writes:

> I'd strongly recommend installing 5.0.  The libc is much less buggy, the
> sh-utils actually log to utmp correctly, XEmacs might even link correctly,
> new versions of the compiler (like egcs) will install without the user
> going through contortions, and in general it's a better system.

While I generally agree with this sentiment, there is one relatively
serious glibc 2.x bug that bites rh5.0: the glibc version shipped with
rh5.0 has an FDMASK macro that is broken.  The effect is that if you
try to use fd_sets with file descriptors >31 then things may go
awfully wrong.  E.g., XF86_TGA terminates with extreme prejudice when
trying to open more than ~30 windows.  More important, emacs seems to
get stuck randomly due to this bug.  The following two changes should
be made to fix the problem:

> <gnu/types.h>:
> 
> old: #define __FDMASK(d)     (1 << ((d) % __NFDBITS))
> new: #define __FDMASK(d)     ((__fd_mask) 1 << ((d) % __NFDBITS))
> 
> <selectbits.h>:
> 
> old: #define __FD_ISSET(d, set) ((set)->fds_bits[__FDELT(d)] & __FDMASK(d))
> new: #define __FD_ISSET(d, set) (((set)->fds_bits[__FDELT(d)] & __FDMASK(d)) != 0)

Of course, you'll need to rebuild packages that use select in order to
get the correct behavior.  I'd think rebuilding XFree86, [X]emacs, and
the networking daemons (certainly inetd) should make the system quite
stable.

On a positive note, rh5.0 seems to be the first Linux/Alpha release
where profiling (with -pg & gprof) works out of the box.  Good work!

	--david



