memory.  This gives you 64meg, the bare minimum I would recommend for a
Multia.

Also, you will need an EXTERNAL case for the HD.

Just so you know this is what I have:

2 Multias

2 Fujitsu drives (above with the adapters) and cables

The adapters drives are used in a baby AT desktop case with a 230w power
supply.  This provides able power AND allows you to expand and place a
CD-ROM in there as well as other drives (Zip, tape, more HD, etc)

Then attach one monitor and one mouse and one keyboard to a switch box. 
(Hey, for $50 with the cables you save a lot of desk place!)

Here is what I use them for:

Multia #1:  NT 4.0 server - not a screamer, but good enough to study for
my Microsoft certifications!  It is also used as the server between the #2
Multia and my Power Computing Mac clone (120MHz 604e)


Multia #2   NT 4.0 Workstation - not a screamer, but good enough to  do
the same as server but for other MCSE exams   AND learn Linux.

Last piece, cheezie, lamer hub, but good enough for a bunch-o boxes on one desk.

Basically, I built a workstation and server sys for under $1600.  I
spec'ed out doing the same from baby AT cases, mo'boards, chips, mem, HD,
netowrk cards, etc on the Intel/AMD/Cyrix side and it cost over $2000!!! 
Instand 20% savings.  And with FX!32 I can, if I want, play the Wintel
games...  But I have Win95 emulators for my Mac so there is no known need
as of yet.

Here is a cost breakdown:

Multia with no HD and no memory, comes with the mouse
$299  x  2 plus shipping = $650 or so

Memory: 8 16 meg true parity simms (64meg per machine): $39 x 8 + s/h = $330

HD: Fujitsu SCA 7200rpm 2.4 Gig drive: $149 x 2 + s/h = $330 or so

Switch box: four systems to each device (VGA, Mouse and keyboard) with
cables to get the monitor to go to 3 computers (2 Multia and 1 Mac) and
keyboard and mouse to go to each Multia:  $50 after S/H

Network:  2 2ft cat-5 cables: $3 total (retail) and cheezy hub: $40

Baby AT Case for drives and other items: $40

SCA to SCSI adapters $30 each 

CD-ROM - 2x for installing RedHat and NT.... since I was only going to use
it one, why get a 24?:  $29

I also purchased the Red Hat 4.2 and Multia manual from Starship Corp for
a total that was under $50.


DONE!  GRAND TOTAL:  $1550 or so.


