dorothee bienzle <bienzle@calc.vet.uga.edu> wrote:

>First, I wish to use Linux. What about hardware?

I just installed Linux on a PC164LX.  It's non-trivial to say the
least (a i686 install takes me 10 minutes).  The 533 MHz alpha we have
is great for number crunching though.  It is about 2.5-3 times FASTER
(for our application) than a dual processor 266 MHz combined.  But the
dual processer pentiums are about 30% cheaper.

>will give the most bang for the buck. Is this true? 

It depends on what you wish to do.  If your simulations are highly
parallelisable, you may benefit from a processor i686 machine instead
of an alpha.

>   * 4.3 GB Hard Drive (IDE)

Hmm, my understanding is that IDE drives are dificult with alpha/Linux.

>Can I do better?

>I'm used to Linux on a Pentium 133. Is the alpha-based version as
>stable?

The alpha Linux came out only recently (1995?).  Even though it has
been out this much, I'd guess it's no more than six months to one year
behind i686 Linux (and I say "behind" in terms of stability of
robustness, not in terms of package available, even though that is the
case for certain packages).

>What experiences have people had with Microway, or would you recommend
>another company?

We got ours from a company called Multiwave, and it has been great thusfar.

>Would I be farther ahead with a dual Pentium Pro?

It really depends. I have two sorts of jobs... one which takes 5
minutes on one input file over several thousand input files. And
another that takes days on one input (non very parallelisable).  In
the former case, the i686 dual-processer machiens win slightly. 

>Also are commercial fortran packages for Linux worth the extra
>expense as compared to f2c and gcc?

I wouldn't know, but f2c isn't great, and gcc is tolerable.  gcc is a
nice compiler, but the DEC OSF/1 binaries compiled statically run on
an alpha/Linux box run about 3 times faster than a gcc compiled
program (all defaults are RedHat 4.2).

If you were to compare gcc-compiled program speeds alone, a SINGLE
Pentium 266 MHz processor runs almost as fast as a 533 MHz
processer. I've heard good things about egcs though.

